@prefix prim: <urn:silmaril:prim:#> .
@prefix silm: <urn:silmaril:entity#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:  <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

# =====================================================================================
# taiji.ttl — the COLIMIT gluing of the Primitive Floor (W2 · SP1, plan Task 5).
# =====================================================================================
# The twin-olog taiji itself: a prim:Primitive is the colimit object over the small
# diagram (FormalType ─realization ρ→ PhysicalEncoding). It is NOT a pair of linked
# fields — it is ONE glued atom that simultaneously IS its formal type (yin) and IS its
# physical encoding (yang), with the realization ρ (formal.ttl → physical.ttl via
# realization.ttl) as the gluing morphism. This is the same colimit shape as the unary
# law's Atlas & Graph colimit and the OSSIE YIN/YANG taiji: yin holds yang and yang holds
# yin. The mutual back-pointer that makes it mutual is prim:interpretAs, already emitted on
# every PhysicalEncoding in physical.ttl; here each Primitive selects one canonical
# realization and asserts the colimit binds both facets. The chosen encoding's
# prim:interpretAs closes back to the SAME formal facet, so the reversibility seal
# physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet → realizesAs → encoding returns the chosen
# encoding — the taiji is fully sealed. AFTER REMEDIATION (spec §9d) EVERY canonical
# Primitive is reflexive: each selects its OWN dedicated encoding whose interpretAs is its
# own formal facet, so the round-trip is a TRUE IDENTITY for all atoms and q_colimit_reflexive
# holds universally. The three former proxy atoms that landed on a shared carrier's DIFFERENT
# formal type (Bignum→Uint128→Natural, Instant→Int64→Integer, Decimal→Ascii→Codepoint) were
# fixed at the source and are superseded by prim:Bignum_Primitive / prim:primitive_Instant /
# prim:Decimal_Primitive over dedicated reflexive carriers; NO Primitive here carries a
# silm:isProvisional marker, because every grounding is resolvable in-scope work (isProvisional
# is reserved for genuinely undecidable Präriehund gaps, of which the floor has none this iteration).
# Corpus-agnostic: pure shape, zero corpus instances.
#
# STEP C (literal Yoneda) is FOLDED INTO this colimit taiji at the end of the file: the atom is
# still the colimit, but its gluing now also carries (a) the Yoneda embedding yo materialised as a
# functor — object map prim:yonedaObject and arrow map prim:YonedaArrow over the STEP A
# representables prim:repr_<A> = Hom(-, A), re-internalised as prim:OlogObject of the presheaf olog
# prim:presheafOlog (Directive 2, ologs of ologs / turtles); (b) ρ made a genuine NATURAL
# transformation rho: yo => R, one prim:RhoComponent per object and one commuting prim:NaturalitySquare
# per subtype arrow (top yo(f) vs bottom the STEP B RealizationTransport rho(f)); and (c) the unary-law
# tie — each representable's Yoneda point prim:id_<A> is evaluated by prim:YonedaEvaluation to yield
# rho(A) = the Frame output carrier, so realization = Yoneda evaluation at the representable, and the
# Frame IS the Yoneda point (prim:Frame prim:isYonedaPoint true). The transported ancestral grounding
# (STEP B prim:AncestralGroundingPath) and this naturality are now part of the gluing the taiji records.
#
# Property-name contract (binding across the floor, matching primitives.queries.sparql):
#   formalFacet   : Primitive -> FormalType         (yin — the formal type it IS)
#   physicalFacet : Primitive -> PhysicalEncoding    (yang — the chosen canonical encoding)
#   gluedBy       : Primitive -> Realization         (the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow that glues them)
#
# Design spec: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-07-w2-primitive-floor-design.md (§1, §6)
# Plan:        docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-07-w2-sp1-primitive-floor.md (Task 5)
# Consumes:    formal.ttl (FormalType) + physical.ttl (PhysicalEncoding) + realization.ttl (Realization)
# Acceptance:  q_colimit_roundtrip stays GREEN (every PhysicalEncoding closes back to a FormalType);
#              the per-Primitive seal is asserted here and enforced by the SHACL PrimitiveShape (Task 6).
# =====================================================================================


# =====================================================================================
# Properties introduced by the taiji colimit
# =====================================================================================

prim:formalFacet a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "formalFacet" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:range prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:comment "The yin edge of the colimit taiji: from a prim:Primitive to the FormalType (formal.ttl) it IS — the mathematical/logical meaning half of the glued atom. Together with prim:physicalFacet it makes a Primitive the colimit of (FormalType ─ρ→ PhysicalEncoding): the atom is not merely associated with its formal type, it is glued to it, so the formal facet is one of the two coordinates the SHACL PrimitiveShape (Task 6) requires and one end of the reversibility seal physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet." .

prim:physicalFacet a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "physicalFacet" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:range prim:PhysicalEncoding ;
    rdfs:comment "The yang edge of the colimit taiji: from a prim:Primitive to the chosen canonical prim:PhysicalEncoding (physical.ttl) it IS — the byte-carrier half of the glued atom. One FormalType realizesAs many encodings (the polymorphism of the realization monad); a Primitive fixes ONE of them as its canonical physical facet, and the realization that produces it is named by prim:gluedBy. The physical facet is the starting point of the mutual round-trip: its prim:interpretAs back-pointer (emitted in physical.ttl) should close back to this Primitive's formalFacet, sealing the taiji." .

prim:gluedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "gluedBy" ;
    rdfs:domain prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:range prim:Realization ;
    rdfs:comment "The gluing morphism of the colimit taiji: from a prim:Primitive to the prim:Realization (realization.ttl) — the Kleisli/Frame arrow ρ — that carries this Primitive's formalFacet into its physicalFacet. gluedBy is what makes the atom a colimit rather than a loose pair: it names the specific ρ whose prim:realizesFrom is the formalFacet and whose prim:encoding / prim:frameOutput is the physicalFacet, and whose prim:frameEffect records the typed cost of that gluing. Selecting the gluedBy realization is exactly selecting which of a polymorphic formal type's many encodings this primitive instance commits to." .


# =====================================================================================
# The Primitive class — the colimit-taiji object (one glued atom = formal ⊕ physical)
# =====================================================================================

prim:Primitive a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive" ;
    rdfs:comment "A primitive — the colimit object of the twin-olog diagram (FormalType ─realization ρ→ PhysicalEncoding), the taiji atom of this whole floor. A Primitive is not two linked things; it is ONE thing glued from two facets: its prim:formalFacet (yin, the mathematical type it IS) and its prim:physicalFacet (yang, the byte-carrier encoding it IS), joined by prim:gluedBy (the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow carrying the typed realization effect). Because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual: the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to the formal facet, so yin holds yang and yang holds yin, and when that back-pointer closes to the same formal facet the reversibility seal physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet → realizesAs → encoding returns the chosen encoding. This mutual-colimit round-trip is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility, and the SHACL PrimitiveShape enforces that every Primitive carries both facets and a gluedBy." .


# =====================================================================================
# Sealed taiji primitives — the chosen canonical encoding's prim:interpretAs closes back
# to the SAME formal facet, so the mutual round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs →
# formalFacet returns the primitive's own formal type. These are the fully-glued atoms.
# =====================================================================================

# ℝ — the litmus primitive: Real glued to Float64 by ℝ⟼Float64 (IEEE754-rounding effect).
# Float64 prim:interpretAs prim:Real, so the seal closes. Float32/Float16/Decimal are the
# other realizesAs arrows of Real (monad polymorphism); Float64 is the canonical facet here.
prim:prim_Real a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive ℝ (taiji: Real ⊕ Float64)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Real ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:Float64 ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Real_Float64 ;
    rdfs:comment "The real-number primitive: yin prim:Real glued to yang prim:Float64 by the ρ arrow prim:realize_Real_Float64. Float64 interpretAs Real, so the mutual round-trip closes exactly — the fully-sealed taiji the what-is-a-number litmus grounds into." .

# ℤ — Integer glued to Int32 by ℤ⟼INT32 (overflow-domain effect). Int32 interpretAs Integer.
# Int8/16/64/128 are the sibling realizesAs arrows; Int32 is the canonical width chosen here
# (matching q_number_realized's ℤ→32-bit litmus assertion).
prim:prim_Integer a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive ℤ (taiji: Integer ⊕ INT32)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Integer ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:Int32 ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Integer_Int32 ;
    rdfs:comment "The integer primitive: yin prim:Integer glued to yang prim:Int32 by prim:realize_Integer_Int32. Int32 interpretAs Integer, so the seal closes; the width-bounded overflow-domain effect is the honest cost carried on the gluing Frame." .

# ℕ — Natural glued to Uint32 by ℕ⟼UINT32 (overflow-domain effect). Uint32 interpretAs Natural.
prim:prim_Natural a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive ℕ (taiji: Natural ⊕ UINT32)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Natural ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:Uint32 ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Natural_Uint32 ;
    rdfs:comment "The natural-number primitive: yin prim:Natural glued to yang prim:Uint32 by prim:realize_Natural_Uint32. Uint32 interpretAs Natural, so the seal closes; the unsigned width's overflow domain is the typed effect on the gluing arrow." .

# Codepoint — glued to UTF-8 by Codepoint⟼UTF-8 (encoding-loss effect). Utf8 interpretAs Codepoint.
# Utf16/Utf32 are the sibling arrows; UTF-8 is the canonical variable-width facet chosen here.
prim:prim_Codepoint a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Codepoint (taiji: Codepoint ⊕ UTF-8)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Codepoint ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:Utf8 ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf8 ;
    rdfs:comment "The Unicode-scalar primitive: yin prim:Codepoint glued to yang prim:Utf8 by prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf8. Utf8 interpretAs Codepoint, so the seal closes; the surrogate/normalization encoding-loss is the typed effect the gluing Frame carries." .

# Bool — glued to BOOLEAN by Bool⟼BOOLEAN (NoEffect: the two truth values map bijectively to
# 0/1). Boolean interpretAs Bool — a lossless, fully-sealed taiji.
prim:prim_Bool a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Bool (taiji: Bool ⊕ BOOLEAN)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Bool ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:Boolean ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Bool_Boolean ;
    rdfs:comment "The Boolean primitive: yin prim:Bool glued to yang prim:Boolean by prim:realize_Bool_Boolean. Boolean interpretAs Bool and the realization carries prim:NoEffect (true/false map bijectively onto 1/0), so this is a lossless, fully-sealed taiji." .

# Unit — the terminal primitive: Unit glued to the zero-width UnitEncoding by the monad unit η
# (NoEffect). UnitEncoding interpretAs Unit — the identity arrow's Frame output still descends to
# the byte floor (zero-width) and the seal closes, with no boundary exemption (unary law).
prim:prim_Unit a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Unit (taiji: Unit ⊕ zero-width UnitEncoding)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Unit ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:UnitEncoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_unit ;
    rdfs:comment "The terminal-Unit primitive: yin prim:Unit glued to yang prim:UnitEncoding (zero bits, zero bytes, the empty product) by the monad unit η prim:realize_unit. UnitEncoding interpretAs Unit, so even the identity arrow's Frame output descends to the byte floor and the seal closes." .



# =====================================================================================
# INTEGRATED colimit prim:Primitive atoms (from basicttl/primitives/frags/*.ttl) — one
# fully-sealed taiji per tower class. Each glues its formalFacet (yin) to its type-specific
# physicalFacet (yang) by gluedBy the frag realization ρ; every physicalFacet's interpretAs
# closes REFLEXIVELY onto the atom's own formalFacet (a TRUE-IDENTITY round-trip, spec §9d).
# The 3 non-reflexive proxy atoms (prim_Bignum/prim_Instant/prim_Decimal) removed above are
# superseded here by prim:Bignum_Primitive / prim:primitive_Instant / prim:Decimal_Primitive.
# =====================================================================================

# ---- (c) The colimit prim:Primitive atom: Bag ⊕ BagMultisetEncoding, glued by ρ -----------------
# The taiji: Bag (yin) ⊕ BagMultisetEncoding (yang), glued by the ρ above. The physical facet's
# interpretAs closes back onto prim:Bag, so the seal is a reflexive TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:BagPrimitive a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Bag (colimit taiji: Bag ⊕ length-prefixed element+multiplicity run)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Bag ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:BagMultisetEncoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:BagRealizesMultiset ;
    rdfs:comment "The bag / multiset primitive — the colimit object over the twin-olog diagram (prim:Bag ─realization ρ→ prim:BagMultisetEncoding) for the single formal type Bag, the taiji atom taiji.ttl did not previously carry for Bag. It is not two linked things but ONE glued atom that simultaneously IS its formal facet prim:formalFacet prim:Bag (yin — the unordered, multiplicity-bearing collection, rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType at the head of the Aggregate tower's unordered branch, the PARENT of prim:Set, and the multiplicity-bearing counterpart of the ordered prim:List and the distinctness-capped prim:Set) and IS its physical facet prim:physicalFacet prim:BagMultisetEncoding (yang — the length-prefixed run of (element, multiplicity-count) pairs of the member encodings over the prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier, a UINT32 distinct-element-cardinality prefix plus that many contiguous (element, UINT32 count) pairs in canonical order, octet-granular and little-endian, descending through prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor), joined by prim:gluedBy prim:BagRealizesMultiset, the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow that carries the yin into the yang bearing the prim:OrderingCanonicalization effect (the canonical pair ordering a bag incurs because it has no intrinsic order, its stored counts keeping every element→multiplicity binding exact). REFLEXIVE SEAL (spec §9d): because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual — the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Bag, so the round-trip prim:physicalFacet → prim:interpretAs → prim:formalFacet returns prim:Bag itself, a TRUE IDENTITY (it lands on Bag, not on its subtype prim:Set nor a bare prim:ByteVector), and the full reversibility loop physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet → realizesAs → encoding returns the chosen encoding. yin holds yang and yang holds yin; this is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility, sealed here for the unordered multiset with NO silm:isProvisional gap because Bag is concrete and directly, exactly grounded on the len+counts-run-over-ByteVector carrier — the canonical multiset atom every multiplicity-bearing unordered-collection formal type of the floor grounds its member-and-count layout into, and the supertype whose unit-capped restriction is the sibling prim:Set." .

# =====================================================================================
# (c) The colimit Primitive atom for prim:Bignum — the glued taiji (formal ⊕ physical).
#     ONE atom that simultaneously IS prim:Bignum (yin) and IS the ByteVector encoding
#     (yang), glued by the realization ρ above. Reflexive round-trip:
#         physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet
#       = prim:Bignum_PhysicalEncoding → prim:Bignum → prim:Bignum   (TRUE IDENTITY).
# =====================================================================================

prim:Bignum_Primitive a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Bignum (taiji: Bignum ⊕ ByteVector(≤128))" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Bignum ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:Bignum_PhysicalEncoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:Bignum_Realization ;
    rdfs:comment "The arbitrary-precision-integer primitive — the colimit object of the twin-olog diagram (prim:Bignum ─ρ→ prim:Bignum_PhysicalEncoding), the taiji atom of this fragment. It is not two linked things but ONE glued atom: its prim:formalFacet is yin (prim:Bignum, the mathematical arbitrary-precision integer, a subclass of prim:Integer that trades a fixed width for unbounded value), its prim:physicalFacet is yang (prim:Bignum_PhysicalEncoding, the ordered prim:ByteVector of ≤128 octets descending Octet → Nibble → Bit to the width-1 floor), and prim:gluedBy names the Kleisli/Frame arrow prim:Bignum_Realization that carries yin into yang bearing the typed prim:MayOverflow cost. Because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual: the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Bignum, so yin holds yang and yang holds yin, and the reversibility seal physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet returns prim:Bignum — a TRUE reflexive identity (spec §9d), fixing the panel-flagged non-reflexive Bignum → UINT128 → Natural round-trip at the source. This is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split ↔ consolidated render reversibility; it is a fully-sealed taiji, carrying NO silm:isProvisional marker because the grounding is resolvable and complete this iteration." .

# ---- (c) the colimit primitive atom (the taiji: formal ⊕ physical, glued by ρ) ------------
prim:prim_BlankNode a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive BlankNode (taiji: BlankNode ⊕ UTF-8 graph-local label)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:BlankNode ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:BlankNodeUtf8LocalId ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_BlankNode_Utf8LocalId ;
    rdfs:comment "The blank-node primitive — the colimit-taiji atom of the Identifier tower's existential leaf: yin prim:BlankNode glued to yang prim:BlankNodeUtf8LocalId by the ρ arrow prim:realize_BlankNode_Utf8LocalId. It is ONE glued object, not a pair of linked fields: it simultaneously IS the formal type BlankNode and IS the UTF-8 graph-local-label encoding, joined by the gluing Frame that carries the prim:EncodingLoss cost. BlankNode is a CONCRETE leaf of the formal tower (a direct subclass of prim:FormalType, not of prim:URN, because it is the one identifier with no Uniform Resource Name) and has no descendants, so it is dual-grounded DIRECTLY by its own realization rather than transitively through a realized ancestor or a canonical descendant — no transitive-grounding link is required, and no silm:isProvisional applies, because a UTF-8 graph-local label is a fully resolvable, in-scope realization rather than a genuinely undecidable gap. The chosen physical facet's prim:interpretAs closes back to prim:BlankNode, so the reversibility seal physicalFacet -> interpretAs -> formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY: the mutual round-trip returns BlankNode itself, not a proxy formal type, making this a fully-sealed taiji. Its bytes descend through prim:Octet to the width-1 prim:Bit floor, so the atom bottoms out at the byte substrate; the graph-local freshening cost is carried honestly on the gluing arrow as prim:EncodingLoss." .

# =====================================================================================
# (c) colimit prim:Primitive atom — the taiji: ONE glued thing = formal ⊕ physical.
#     formalFacet + physicalFacet + gluedBy. The seal closes reflexively onto prim:Blob.
# =====================================================================================
prim:prim_Blob a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Blob (taiji: Blob ⊕ ByteVector(≤128 opaque octets))" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Blob ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:BlobOpaqueByteVector ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Blob_Opaque ;
    rdfs:comment "The opaque-binary primitive — the colimit-taiji atom for prim:Blob and the object this fragment exists to build. It is ONE glued thing, not a linked pair: its yin prim:formalFacet prim:Blob (the abstract opaque-content type of the binary/opaque tower) is glued to its yang prim:physicalFacet prim:BlobOpaqueByteVector (the ordered ByteVector of ≤128 raw octets that descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor) by prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Blob_Opaque, the NoEffect verbatim byte-identity Kleisli/Frame arrow. Because prim:BlobOpaqueByteVector prim:interpretAs prim:Blob, the mutual-colimit round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet returns prim:Blob EXACTLY — a true reflexive identity (spec §9d), so the taiji is fully sealed and this atom carries no silm:isProvisional (unlike the proxy-landing Instant/Decimal atoms of the monolithic taiji). Blob simultaneously IS its formal opaque type and IS the byte vector of raw octets it realizes into; that mutual gluing (yin holds yang, yang holds yin) is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility. prim:Hash is a taxonomic fixed-width subtype of prim:Blob, but it carries its OWN direct grounding — its own realization prim:realize_Hash_Digest onto its own dedicated reflexive carrier prim:HashDigestByteVector (whose prim:interpretAs closes back to prim:Hash) and its own primitive prim:prim_Hash — so the Blob subtyping is taxonomic only, an additional §9a coverage path, NOT Hash's grounding mechanism." .

# ---- (c) the colimit taiji atom: the glued Bool primitive ---------------------------
# The one glued thing that simultaneously IS prim:Bool (yin) and IS prim:BoolUint8 (yang),
# joined by prim:realize_Bool_Uint8. Because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual: the
# physical facet's prim:interpretAs closes back to prim:Bool, so
# physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet returns prim:Bool exactly — a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:prim_Bool_Uint8 a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Bool (taiji: Bool ⊕ UINT8(0/1))" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Bool ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:BoolUint8 ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Bool_Uint8 ;
    rdfs:comment "The Boolean primitive as the colimit object of the twin-olog diagram (FormalType ─realization ρ→ PhysicalEncoding) — one glued taiji atom, not a pair of linked fields. It simultaneously IS its formal facet prim:Bool (yin — the two-valued truth type, a subclass of prim:Kleene3 with the unknown value forbidden) and IS its physical facet prim:BoolUint8 (yang — the UINT8(0/1) one-octet code cell), joined by the gluing morphism prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Bool_Uint8 (the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow carrying prim:NoEffect). Because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual: the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Bool, so the reversibility seal physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet returns prim:Bool exactly — a true reflexive identity and a fully-sealed taiji, not a provisional proxy that would land on a different formal type. prim:Bool is a concrete, resolvable leaf of the Boolean tower, so this atom is realized directly with no isProvisional dodge and no transitive-grounding indirection; the seal closes losslessly and byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor, the base unit of the whole primitive floor." .

# =====================================================================================
# (c) The colimit primitive atom — CRS glued to its UINT32 EPSG encoding (TRUE IDENTITY).
# =====================================================================================
prim:prim_CRS a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive CRS (taiji: CRS ⊕ UINT32 EPSG code)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:CRS ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:CRS_EpsgUint32 ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_CRS_EpsgUint32 ;
    rdfs:comment "The coordinate-reference-system primitive: the colimit object of the twin-olog diagram (prim:CRS ─realization ρ→ prim:CRS_EpsgUint32) — one glued taiji atom, not two linked fields. Its prim:formalFacet is yin prim:CRS (the geospatial-frame type it IS), its prim:physicalFacet is yang prim:CRS_EpsgUint32 (the UINT32 EPSG byte-carrier encoding it IS), and prim:gluedBy names the ρ arrow prim:realize_CRS_EpsgUint32 that carries yin into yang with the typed effect prim:NoEffect. Because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual: the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:CRS, so the reversibility seal physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet returns prim:CRS — a TRUE IDENTITY (reflexive) round-trip, exactly the sealed-taiji condition of spec §9d, with no proxy carrier and no silm:isProvisional dodge. This mutual-colimit round-trip is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility; the atom simultaneously is its formal geospatial type and its physical four-octet byte descent to the prim:Bit floor, and it seeds the S/O/P-tower CRS sub-project (#3) that grounds coordinate geometry against this reference-system atom." .

# =====================================================================================
# (c) The colimit primitive atom — Categorical glued to its UINT32 label (TRUE IDENTITY).
# =====================================================================================
# The taiji: Categorical (yin) ⊕ Categorical_Uint32Index (yang), glued by the ρ above. The physical
# facet's interpretAs closes back onto prim:Categorical, so the seal is a reflexive TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:prim_Categorical a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Categorical (colimit taiji: Categorical ⊕ UINT32 identity-label code)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Categorical ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:Categorical_Uint32Index ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Categorical_Uint32Index ;
    rdfs:comment "The categorical-enumeration primitive — the colimit object over the twin-olog diagram (prim:Categorical ─realization ρ→ prim:Categorical_Uint32Index) for the single formal type of an unordered finite named-member set. It is not two linked things but ONE glued atom that simultaneously IS its formal facet prim:formalFacet prim:Categorical (yin — a finite, explicitly named set of members with NO meaningful order, rdfs:subClassOf prim:Enumeration ⊂ prim:FormalType, the unordered complement of prim:Ordinal) and IS its physical facet prim:physicalFacet prim:Categorical_Uint32Index (yang — the unsigned 32-bit identity-label carrier, 32 bits / 4 bytes, descending through prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor), joined by prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Categorical_Uint32Index, the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow that carries the yin into the yang bearing the exact, lossless prim:NoEffect. CONCRETE-LEAF WITNESS (spec §9a direct-for-concrete): prim:Categorical is realized directly (an unordered finite named set is a fully determined, directly-instantiable type — Präriehund honesty names the ordered/unordered fork as the two sub-objects Ordinal/Categorical rather than picking a side), so no ancestral proxy is used for the seal and no silm:isProvisional gap is admitted. TRANSITIVE-GROUNDING LINK (spec §9a self-or-ancestor): Categorical remains dual-grounded two ways — DIRECTLY by this atom, and TRANSITIVELY through its realized ancestor prim:Enumeration (Categorical ⊂ Enumeration in formal.ttl; Enumeration realized by prim:realize_Enumeration_Uint32Index / prim:realize_Enumeration_Index onto prim:Enumeration_Uint32Index / prim:EnumerationIndex) — an ancestral grounding STEP B now MATERIALISES as the real edge prim:agp_Categorical_Enumeration (prim:ancestralViaArrow prim:sub_Categorical_Enumeration, prim:ancestralViaTransport prim:rt_sub_Categorical_Enumeration, prim:ancestralLandsIn prim:Enumeration_Uint32Index = rho(prim:Enumeration)), so the once-prose 'also grounded through the ancestor' is now a resolvable graph fact, so both the direct and the ancestral coverage paths hold while the round-trip is still a genuine identity; the direct path is the canonical one this Primitive commits to. REFLEXIVE SEAL (spec §9d): because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual — the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Categorical, so the round-trip prim:physicalFacet → prim:interpretAs → prim:formalFacet returns prim:Categorical itself, a TRUE IDENTITY (NOT prim:Natural, whose UINT32 it structurally mirrors; NOT the ancestral generic prim:Enumeration, whose UINT32 code it structurally mirrors while carrying its OWN dedicated prim:Categorical_Uint32Index carrier rather than an inherited one; and NOT a shared proxy), and the full reversibility loop physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet → realizesAs → encoding returns the chosen encoding. yin holds yang and yang holds yin all the way down to the byte substrate; this is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility, sealed here for the unordered categorical enumeration with NO silm:isProvisional gap because Categorical is concrete and directly, exactly grounded on the UINT32 identity-label carrier — it seeds the categorical-label structures the Atlas ring and the mole-of-glossaries polysemy sub-projects build atop this Enum tower." .

# ---- (c) the colimit taiji atom: the glued Codepoint primitive ----------------------
# The one glued thing that simultaneously IS prim:Codepoint (yin) and IS prim:CodepointUtf32
# (yang), joined by prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf32Scalar. Because the gluing is a colimit it is
# mutual: the physical facet's prim:interpretAs closes back to prim:Codepoint, so
# physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet returns prim:Codepoint exactly — a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:prim_Codepoint_Utf32 a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Codepoint (taiji: Codepoint ⊕ UTF-32 scalar)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Codepoint ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:CodepointUtf32 ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf32Scalar ;
    rdfs:comment "The Unicode-scalar primitive as the colimit object of the twin-olog diagram (FormalType ─realization ρ→ PhysicalEncoding) — one glued taiji atom, not a pair of linked fields. It simultaneously IS its formal facet prim:Codepoint (yin — the abstract Unicode scalar value in U+0000..U+10FFFF, the atom of the Text tower, whose composite neighbours prim:Grapheme and prim:String are SIBLINGS built from codepoints, not subtypes) and IS its physical facet prim:CodepointUtf32 (yang — the fixed 32-bit UTF-32 scalar byte carrier, descending through 4 prim:Octet occurrences to the prim:Bit floor), joined by the gluing morphism prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Codepoint_Utf32Scalar (the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow carrying prim:EncodingLoss). Because the gluing is a colimit it is MUTUAL: the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Codepoint, so the reversibility seal physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet returns the primitive's own formal type prim:Codepoint exactly — a TRUE reflexive IDENTITY and a fully-sealed taiji, not a provisional proxy that would land on prim:Natural via a shared UINT32 carrier. prim:Codepoint is a concrete, directly-realizable leaf of the Text tower, so this atom is realized directly with its OWN encoding and realization — NO silm:isProvisional dodge and NO transitive-grounding indirection (spec §9a/§9b/§9d) — and being atomic (non-composite) it has no member encodings; the seal closes and byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor, the base unit of the whole primitive floor." .

# ---- (c) The colimit prim:Primitive atom: Complex ⊕ ComplexProductEncoding, glued by ρ -----------
# The taiji: Complex (yin) ⊕ ComplexProductEncoding (yang), glued by the ρ above. The physical
# facet's interpretAs closes back onto prim:Complex, so the seal is a reflexive TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:ComplexPrimitive a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive ℂ (colimit taiji: Complex ⊕ Product(Float64 real, Float64 imaginary))" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Complex ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:ComplexProductEncoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:ComplexRealizesProduct ;
    rdfs:comment "The complex-number primitive — the colimit object over the twin-olog diagram (prim:Complex ─realization ρ→ prim:ComplexProductEncoding) for the single formal type ℂ. It is not two linked things but ONE glued atom that simultaneously IS its formal facet prim:formalFacet prim:Complex (yin — the complex numbers ℂ, the top of the number tower, the join of prim:Real ⊂ prim:Complex and prim:Imaginary ⊂ prim:Complex) and IS its physical facet prim:physicalFacet prim:ComplexProductEncoding (yang — the real·imaginary product of two IEEE-754 Float64 members over the prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier, total 128 bits / 16 bytes, descending through prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor), joined by prim:gluedBy prim:ComplexRealizesProduct, the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow that carries the yin into the yang bearing the finite-grid prim:RoundingIEEE754 effect on both coordinates. REFLEXIVE SEAL (spec §9d): because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual — the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Complex, so the round-trip prim:physicalFacet → prim:interpretAs → prim:formalFacet returns prim:Complex itself, a TRUE IDENTITY, and the full reversibility loop physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet → realizesAs → encoding returns the chosen encoding. yin holds yang and yang holds yin; this is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility, sealed here for ℂ with NO silm:isProvisional gap because Complex is concrete and directly, exactly grounded on the pair-of-doubles carrier — the ℂ coordinate at the top of the what-is-a-number litmus chain ℕ⊂ℤ⊂ℚ⊂ℝ⊂ℂ." .

# ---- (c) The colimit prim:Primitive atom: Coordinate ⊕ CoordinateFloat64Encoding, glued by ρ ------
# The taiji: Coordinate (yin) ⊕ CoordinateFloat64Encoding (yang), glued by the ρ above. The physical
# facet's interpretAs closes back onto prim:Coordinate, so the seal is a reflexive TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:CoordinatePrimitive a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Coordinate (colimit taiji: Coordinate ⊕ IEEE-754 double axis position)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Coordinate ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:CoordinateFloat64Encoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:CoordinateRealizesFloat64 ;
    rdfs:comment "The coordinate primitive — the colimit object over the twin-olog diagram (prim:Coordinate ─realization ρ→ prim:CoordinateFloat64Encoding) for the single formal type of one geospatial axis position. It is not two linked things but ONE glued atom that simultaneously IS its formal facet prim:formalFacet prim:Coordinate (yin — a single scalar position along one axis of a coordinate reference system, rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType, the atom of the geospatial tower and the parent of prim:Latitude and prim:Longitude) and IS its physical facet prim:physicalFacet prim:CoordinateFloat64Encoding (yang — the single IEEE-754 Float64 axis carrier, 64 bits / 8 bytes, descending through prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor), joined by prim:gluedBy prim:CoordinateRealizesFloat64, the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow that carries the yin into the yang bearing the finite-grid prim:RoundingIEEE754 effect. COMPONENT-OF-PRODUCT WITNESS: this scalar primitive is the Real-valued component member from which the Product(Real…) coordinate tuple of a prim:Point geometry is assembled — the position is the product, the coordinate is the member — so the Point carrier reuses this atom's encoding for each of its axes rather than inventing a new scalar (the honest reading of the Product(Real…) hint). DESCENDANT SUBTYPING (spec §9a, taxonomic not grounding): prim:Latitude and prim:Longitude are formal subtypes of prim:Coordinate, but each carries its OWN direct grounding — its own realization (prim:realize_Latitude_Float64 / prim:realize_Longitude_Float64) onto its own dedicated reflexive carrier (prim:LatitudeFloat64 / prim:LongitudeFloat64, each whose prim:interpretAs closes back to prim:Latitude / prim:Longitude respectively) and its own primitive (prim:prim_Latitude / prim:prim_Longitude). Their bounded ±90° / ±180° ranges are facets carried on those dedicated carriers; the ancestry to prim:Coordinate is taxonomic subtyping only, an additional §9a coverage path, NOT their grounding mechanism. REFLEXIVE SEAL (spec §9d): because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual — the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Coordinate, so the round-trip prim:physicalFacet → prim:interpretAs → prim:formalFacet returns prim:Coordinate itself, a TRUE IDENTITY (NOT prim:Real, whose Float64 it structurally mirrors), and the full reversibility loop physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet → realizesAs → encoding returns the chosen encoding. yin holds yang and yang holds yin; this is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility, sealed here for the geospatial coordinate axis with NO silm:isProvisional gap because Coordinate is concrete and directly, exactly grounded on the Real-axis double carrier." .

# =====================================================================================
# (c) The colimit Primitive atom — one glued taiji (formal ⊕ physical)
# =====================================================================================
# formalFacet (yin) + physicalFacet (yang) + gluedBy (the ρ arrow). The physicalFacet's
# prim:interpretAs closes back to the formalFacet, so the mutual round-trip is a TRUE IDENTITY:
# physicalFacet → interpretAs == formalFacet == prim:Currency (spec §9d).
prim:prim_Currency a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Currency (colimit taiji: Currency ⊕ 3×CHAR8 ISO-4217 alphabetic code)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Currency ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:CurrencyIso4217Alpha ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Currency_Iso4217Alpha ;
    rdfs:comment "The currency primitive — the colimit object over the twin-olog diagram (prim:Currency ─realization ρ→ prim:CurrencyIso4217Alpha) for the single formal type Currency. It is not two linked things but ONE glued atom that simultaneously IS its formal facet prim:formalFacet prim:Currency (yin — a monetary unit of measure such as an ISO-4217 code USD/EUR/JPY, a subclass of prim:UnitOfMeasure and thereby of prim:FormalType in the Quantity tower, the reference magnitude for the dimension of monetary value) and IS its physical facet prim:physicalFacet prim:CurrencyIso4217Alpha (yang — the fixed 3×CHAR8 alphabetic code, three prim:Char8 members each interpreting to a prim:Codepoint, over the prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier, descending through prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor), joined by prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Currency_Iso4217Alpha, the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow that carries the yin into the yang bearing the exact, lossless prim:NoEffect cost. REFLEXIVE SEAL (spec §9d): because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual — the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Currency, so the round-trip prim:physicalFacet → prim:interpretAs → prim:formalFacet returns prim:Currency itself, a TRUE IDENTITY, and the full reversibility loop physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet → realizesAs → encoding returns the chosen encoding. TRANSITIVE GROUNDING (spec §9a): Currency is dual-grounded twice over — directly on its own ISO-4217 carrier here (direct-for-concrete), and by ancestry through prim:UnitOfMeasure, whose realized fragment records the downward link to this leaf — so no abstract-supertype proxy is needed and this atom carries NO silm:isProvisional gap (spec §9b: isProvisional is reserved for genuinely undecidable Präriehund gaps, never for resolvable in-scope work). yin holds yang and yang holds yin; this is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility, sealed here for Currency and grounded recursively through its member encodings to the byte substrate." .

# =====================================================================================
