#     ONE atom that simultaneously IS prim:Decimal (yin) and IS the packed-BCD ByteVector
#     encoding (yang), glued by the realization ρ above. Reflexive round-trip:
#         physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet
#       = prim:Decimal_PhysicalEncoding → prim:Decimal → prim:Decimal   (TRUE IDENTITY).
# =====================================================================================

prim:Decimal_Primitive a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Decimal (colimit taiji: Decimal ⊕ ByteVector(packed BCD base-10))" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Decimal ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:Decimal_PhysicalEncoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:Decimal_Realization ;
    rdfs:comment "The base-10 fixed-point primitive — the colimit object of the twin-olog diagram (prim:Decimal ─realization ρ→ prim:Decimal_PhysicalEncoding), the taiji atom of this fragment. It is not two linked things but ONE glued atom: its prim:formalFacet is yin (prim:Decimal, the exact base-10 fixed-point number, rdfs:subClassOf prim:Rational and the exact-decimal sibling of prim:Bignum, the type that exists because binary floats cannot hold 0.1 exactly), its prim:physicalFacet is yang (prim:Decimal_PhysicalEncoding, the packed-BCD prim:ByteVector whose octets each hold two base-10 digits as two prim:Nibble half-octets plus a sign nibble and a decimal scale, descending ByteVector → Octet → Nibble → Bit to the width-1 floor), and prim:gluedBy names the Kleisli/Frame arrow prim:Decimal_Realization that carries yin into yang bearing the typed prim:Base10Precision cost. REFLEXIVE SEAL (spec §9d): because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual — the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Decimal (NOT to prim:Codepoint, the way the shared prim:Ascii lexical proxy the panel flagged did), so the reversibility seal physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet returns prim:Decimal itself — a TRUE reflexive identity — and the full loop physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet → realizesAs → encoding returns the chosen encoding. yin holds yang and yang holds yin; this is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split ↔ consolidated render reversibility, sealed here for the base-10 sibling of the what-is-a-number Number tower. It is a fully-sealed taiji carrying NO silm:isProvisional marker because prim:Decimal is concrete and its base-10 grounding is resolvable and complete this iteration, fixing the §9d non-reflexive Decimal round-trip at the source." .

