    rdfs:comment "The RDF-graph primitive — the colimit object of the twin-olog diagram (prim:RDFGraph ─realization ρ→ prim:RDFGraphTurtleEncoding), the taiji atom for the RDF-term tower's top type and the atom taiji.ttl did not previously carry for RDFGraph. It is ONE glued thing, not two linked ones: its yin prim:formalFacet is prim:RDFGraph (the mathematical/logical type it IS — a set of Triples), its yang prim:physicalFacet is prim:RDFGraphTurtleEncoding (the octet-granular Turtle/N-Triples UTF-8 byte stream it IS, descending prim:Stream → prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor), and prim:gluedBy prim:realize_RDFGraph_TurtleFrag is the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow that carries the formal facet into the physical facet while recording the typed prim:EncodingLoss cost of that gluing. Because the gluing is a colimit it is MUTUAL: the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:RDFGraph, so yin holds yang and yang holds yin, and the reversibility seal physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE reflexive IDENTITY on prim:RDFGraph (it lands on the graph's own formal type, never on a proxy such as prim:Triple, prim:Set, or prim:Codepoint), which is why this atom is fully sealed and carries no silm:isProvisional flag. This per-primitive mutual round-trip is the split-projection instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility: this very fragment is the split form of the RDFGraph atom, and consolidating it back reproduces the same formal facet, physical carrier, gluing arrow, and byte descent without inventing or renaming a coordinate." .

# ---- (c) The colimit prim:Primitive atom: Rational ⊕ RationalProductEncoding, glued by ρ ---------
# The taiji: Rational (yin) ⊕ RationalProductEncoding (yang), glued by the ρ above. The physical
# facet's interpretAs closes back onto prim:Rational, so the seal is a reflexive TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:RationalPrimitive a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive ℚ (colimit taiji: Rational ⊕ Product(INT32 numerator, INT32 denominator))" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Rational ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:RationalProductEncoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:RationalRealizesProduct ;
    rdfs:comment "The rational-number primitive — the colimit object over the twin-olog diagram (prim:Rational ─realization ρ→ prim:RationalProductEncoding) for the single formal type ℚ. It is not two linked things but ONE glued atom that simultaneously IS its formal facet prim:formalFacet prim:Rational (yin — the ratios p/q of an Integer numerator and non-zero Integer denominator, rdfs:subClassOf prim:Real, superclass of prim:Integer and sibling-parent of prim:Decimal) and IS its physical facet prim:physicalFacet prim:RationalProductEncoding (yang — the numerator·denominator product of two two's-complement INT32 members over the prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier, descending through prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor), joined by prim:gluedBy prim:RationalRealizesProduct, the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow that carries the yin into the yang bearing the width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain effect. REFLEXIVE SEAL (spec §9d): because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual — the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Rational, so the round-trip prim:physicalFacet → prim:interpretAs → prim:formalFacet returns prim:Rational itself, a TRUE IDENTITY, and the full reversibility loop physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet → realizesAs → encoding returns the chosen encoding. yin holds yang and yang holds yin; this is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility, sealed here for ℚ with NO silm:isProvisional gap because Rational is concrete and directly, exactly grounded on the product-of-integers carrier — the ℚ coordinate of the what-is-a-number litmus chain ℕ⊂ℤ⊂ℚ⊂ℝ⊂ℂ." .

# ---- (c) The colimit prim:Primitive atom: Real ⊕ RealFloat64Encoding, glued by ρ -----
prim:RealPrimitive a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive ℝ (taiji: Real ⊕ IEEE-754 double)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Real ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:RealFloat64Encoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:RealRealizesFloat64 ;
    rdfs:comment "The real-number primitive — the colimit object of the twin-olog diagram (prim:Real ─ρ→ prim:RealFloat64Encoding), one glued taiji atom rather than two linked fields. Its prim:formalFacet is yin prim:Real (the mathematical type ℝ it IS, sitting Real ⊂ Complex and Real ⊃ Rational in the number tower); its prim:physicalFacet is yang prim:RealFloat64Encoding (the IEEE-754 double byte carrier it IS, descending through prim:Octet to the prim:Bit floor); and prim:gluedBy names the ρ arrow prim:RealRealizesFloat64 that carries yin into yang bearing the prim:RoundingIEEE754 effect. Because the gluing is a colimit it is MUTUAL: the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Real, so the reversibility seal physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet returns the primitive's own formal type — a TRUE IDENTITY round-trip, fully sealed. This is a concrete, directly-realizable number type, so the atom carries NO silm:isProvisional (spec §9a/§9b/§9d): it is the litmus primitive the what-is-a-number chain ℕ⊂ℤ⊂ℚ⊂ℝ⊂ℂ grounds its real coordinate into." .

# ---- (c) The colimit prim:Primitive atom: Set ⊕ SetSortedEncoding, glued by ρ -------------------
# The taiji: Set (yin) ⊕ SetSortedEncoding (yang), glued by the ρ above. The physical facet's
# interpretAs closes back onto prim:Set, so the seal is a reflexive TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:SetPrimitive a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Set (colimit taiji: Set ⊕ canonically sorted length-prefixed distinct run)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Set ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:SetSortedEncoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:SetRealizesSorted ;
    rdfs:comment "The set primitive — the colimit object over the twin-olog diagram (prim:Set ─realization ρ→ prim:SetSortedEncoding) for the single formal type Set, the taiji atom taiji.ttl did not previously carry for Set. It is not two linked things but ONE glued atom that simultaneously IS its formal facet prim:formalFacet prim:Set (yin — the unordered, duplicate-free collection, rdfs:subClassOf prim:Bag in the Aggregate tower, the shape behind an prim:RDFGraph, and the unordered counterpart of the ordered prim:List and the multiplicity-bearing prim:Bag) and IS its physical facet prim:physicalFacet prim:SetSortedEncoding (yang — the canonically sorted, length-prefixed, DISTINCT run of the member encodings over the prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier, a UINT32 cardinality prefix plus that many contiguous distinct element encodings in canonical sort order, octet-granular and little-endian, descending through prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor), joined by prim:gluedBy prim:SetRealizesSorted, the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow that carries the yin into the yang bearing the prim:OrderingCanonicalization effect (the canonical sort a set incurs because it has no intrinsic order). REFLEXIVE SEAL (spec §9d): because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual — the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Set, so the round-trip prim:physicalFacet → prim:interpretAs → prim:formalFacet returns prim:Set itself, a TRUE IDENTITY (it lands on Set, not on its supertype prim:Bag nor a bare prim:ByteVector), and the full reversibility loop physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet → realizesAs → encoding returns the chosen encoding. yin holds yang and yang holds yin; this is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility, sealed here for the unordered set with NO silm:isProvisional gap because Set is concrete and directly, exactly grounded on the sorted-distinct-run-over-ByteVector carrier — the canonical set atom every unordered-collection formal type of the floor (and the RDF-graph-as-set-of-triples shape) grounds its member layout into." .

# ---- (c) The colimit Primitive atom — the glued taiji (formal ⊕ physical) -----------
prim:prim_String a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive String (taiji: String ⊕ UTF-8 octet ByteVector)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:String ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:StringUtf8ByteVector ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_String_Utf8ByteVector ;
    rdfs:comment "The String primitive — the colimit object of the twin-olog diagram (prim:String ─ρ→ prim:StringUtf8ByteVector), one glued taiji atom that simultaneously IS its formal type (yin prim:formalFacet prim:String) and IS its physical encoding (yang prim:physicalFacet prim:StringUtf8ByteVector), joined by prim:gluedBy prim:realize_String_Utf8ByteVector (the Frame arrow carrying the surrogate/normalization prim:EncodingLoss as its typed cost). Because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual: the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:String, so the reversibility seal physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet is a TRUE IDENTITY — it returns prim:String, the atom's own formal facet, reflexively, not a proxy such as prim:Codepoint (spec §9d). String is concrete and directly realized, so this atom is fully sealed with no isProvisional dodge; it is the Text-tower instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility, the whole String atom rendered as exactly one precise semantic thing in one file." .

# ---- (c) the colimit prim:Primitive atom — one glued thing (formal ⊕ physical) ---------------
prim:prim_Sum a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Sum (taiji: Sum ⊕ UINT8-tag+payload coproduct encoding)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Sum ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:SumTaggedUnionEncoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Sum_taggedUnion ;
    rdfs:comment "The sum/coproduct primitive — the colimit atom of the twin-olog diagram (FormalType ─realization ρ→ PhysicalEncoding) for prim:Sum: ONE glued thing that simultaneously IS its formal facet prim:Sum (yin — the tagged-union type of the Algebraic-constructor tower, categorical dual of the prim:Tuple product and supertype of prim:Optional) and IS its physical facet prim:SumTaggedUnionEncoding (yang — the UINT8-discriminant-tag-plus-selected-payload byte carrier, octet-granular over a prim:ByteVector whose prim:Octet occurrences byteDescend through prim:Nibble to the width-1 prim:Bit floor), joined by prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Sum_taggedUnion — the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow carrying the honest prim:NoEffect (lossless tag+payload framing). Because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual: the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Sum, so the reversibility seal physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet returns prim:Sum EXACTLY — a true reflexive identity (spec §9d), the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility. There is no proxy carrier and no silm:isProvisional: the coproduct is directly realizable through its most canonical concrete form (a UINT8 tag selecting one payload), so this taiji is fully sealed rather than a documented gap — a concrete Algebraic-tower primitive standing beside prim:prim_Real / prim:prim_Bool as a lossless, fully-grounded atom of the floor." .

# ---- (c) The colimit prim:Primitive atom: Tensor ⊕ TensorShapedBlockEncoding, glued by ρ ---------
# The taiji: Tensor (yin) ⊕ TensorShapedBlockEncoding (yang), glued by the ρ above. The physical
# facet's interpretAs closes back onto prim:Tensor, so the seal is a reflexive TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:TensorPrimitive a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Tensor (colimit taiji: Tensor ⊕ shape header + row-major dense block)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Tensor ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:TensorShapedBlockEncoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:TensorRealizesShapedBlock ;
    rdfs:comment "The tensor primitive — the colimit object over the twin-olog diagram (prim:Tensor ─realization ρ→ prim:TensorShapedBlockEncoding) for the single formal type Tensor, and the LOAD-BEARING per-atom value shape the AOB tensor block grounds into. It is not two linked things but ONE glued atom that simultaneously IS its formal facet prim:formalFacet prim:Tensor (yin — the n-dimensional numeric array with explicit shape and stride, a DIRECT rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType in the Aggregate tower, the arbitrary-rank generalisation of the rank-1 prim:Vector and kept distinct from the flat prim:List/prim:Vector precisely so its shape metadata stays first-class) and IS its physical facet prim:physicalFacet prim:TensorShapedBlockEncoding (yang — the shape header of a UINT32 rank plus per-axis extents followed by a dense row-major component block over the prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier, octet-granular and little-endian, descending through prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor, whose members are the prim:Uint32 extents and the element-dtype component encodings prim:Float32/prim:Float64/prim:Int32), joined by prim:gluedBy prim:TensorRealizesShapedBlock, the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow that carries the yin into the yang bearing the lossless-framing prim:NoEffect. REFLEXIVE SEAL (spec §9d): because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual — the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Tensor, so the round-trip prim:physicalFacet → prim:interpretAs → prim:formalFacet returns prim:Tensor itself, a TRUE IDENTITY (it lands on Tensor, not on its rank-1 prim:Vector isomorph nor a bare prim:ByteVector), and the full reversibility loop physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet → realizesAs → encoding returns the chosen encoding. yin holds yang and yang holds yin; this is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility, sealed here for the n-dimensional array with NO silm:isProvisional gap because Tensor is concrete and directly, exactly grounded on the shape-header-plus-row-major-block-over-ByteVector carrier — the shaped dense-block atom the AOB tensor/aggregate block and every n-dimensional numeric formal type of the floor grounds its component layout into." .

# =====================================================================================
# (c) The colimit prim:Primitive atom (the taiji) — Time ⊕ INT64 nanos, glued by ρ.
# =====================================================================================
prim:prim_Time a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Time (taiji: Time ⊕ INT64 nanos-since-midnight)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Time ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:TimeInt64Nanos ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Time_Int64 ;
    rdfs:comment "The time-of-day primitive — the colimit object of the twin-olog diagram (prim:Time ─ρ→ prim:TimeInt64Nanos), one glued taiji atom that simultaneously IS its formal type (yin prim:formalFacet prim:Time) and its physical encoding (yang prim:physicalFacet prim:TimeInt64Nanos), joined by prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Time_Int64 which carries the width-bounded prim:OverflowDomain effect on its gluing Frame. Because prim:Time is a genuinely CONCRETE tower type — a direct subclass of prim:FormalType in formal.ttl with its own canonical INT64-nanos carrier — it is dual-grounded DIRECTLY: no transitive grounding through a realized ancestor or descendant, and no silm:isProvisional dodge is warranted (spec §9a/§9b; isProvisional is reserved for genuinely undecidable Praeriehund gaps, which a resolvable temporal scalar is not). The gluing is a colimit and therefore mutual: the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Time, so the reversibility seal physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet returns prim:Time exactly — a fully-sealed, REFLEXIVE round-trip identity (spec §9d), the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility." .

# ---- (c) The colimit primitive atom (the glued taiji object) -------------------------
# The taiji: prim:prim_Triple is the colimit object over (prim:Triple ─ρ→ prim:TripleNTriplesProduct).
# It is ONE glued atom that simultaneously IS its formal type (formalFacet) and IS its physical
# encoding (physicalFacet), joined by prim:gluedBy (the ρ arrow above). Reflexive seal (§9d):
#   physicalFacet(prim_Triple) = prim:TripleNTriplesProduct
#   prim:TripleNTriplesProduct prim:interpretAs prim:Triple
#   formalFacet(prim_Triple)   = prim:Triple
#   ⇒ physicalFacet → interpretAs == formalFacet   (TRUE identity, no proxy).
prim:prim_Triple a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Triple (colimit taiji: Triple ⊕ N-Triples UTF-8 Product(Subject, Predicate, Object))" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Triple ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:TripleNTriplesProduct ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Triple_NTriplesProduct ;
    rdfs:comment "The RDF-triple primitive — the colimit object over the twin-olog diagram (prim:Triple ─realization ρ→ prim:TripleNTriplesProduct), the taiji atom for the RDF-term tower's atomic subject-predicate-object proposition. It is not two linked things but ONE glued atom that simultaneously IS its formal facet prim:formalFacet prim:Triple (yin — the ordered S-P-O statement, a direct subclass of prim:FormalType; a prim:Quad is a wider product and prim:RDFGraph a set over it, so Triple has no tower supertype but FormalType and no descendant to realize through) and IS its physical facet prim:physicalFacet prim:TripleNTriplesProduct (yang — the N-Triples UTF-8 serialization of the Product(Subject, Predicate, Object), descending through prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor), joined by prim:gluedBy prim:realize_Triple_NTriplesProduct, the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow carrying the prim:EncodingLoss cost of the gluing. The atom's three member coordinates are themselves realized carriers — Subject and Object as URN or blank-node identifiers (prim:UrnUtf8 / prim:BlankNodeLabel) or, for the Object, a typed literal (prim:LiteralUtf8), the Predicate always a URN — held by the unary law as independent S/O/P towers, never three interchangeable strings. REFLEXIVE SEAL (spec §9d): because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual — the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Triple, so the round-trip prim:physicalFacet → prim:interpretAs → prim:formalFacet returns prim:Triple itself, a TRUE IDENTITY (never a proxy such as Codepoint or a single member URN), and the full reversibility loop physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet → realizesAs → encoding returns the chosen encoding. yin holds yang and yang holds yin; this is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility, sealed here for Triple with NO silm:isProvisional gap because Triple is concrete and directly grounded on its own N-Triples product carrier." .

# ---- (c) The colimit prim:Primitive atom: Tuple ⊕ TupleProductEncoding, glued by ρ --------------
# The taiji: Tuple (yin) ⊕ TupleProductEncoding (yang), glued by the ρ above. The physical facet's
# interpretAs closes back onto prim:Tuple, so the seal is a reflexive TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:TuplePrimitive a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Tuple (colimit taiji: Tuple ⊕ ordered member-encoding concatenation)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Tuple ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:TupleProductEncoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:TupleRealizesProduct ;
    rdfs:comment "The tuple / finite-product primitive — the colimit object over the twin-olog diagram (prim:Tuple ─realization ρ→ prim:TupleProductEncoding) for the single formal type Tuple. It is not two linked things but ONE glued atom that simultaneously IS its formal facet prim:formalFacet prim:Tuple (yin — the ordered, fixed-arity, heterogeneous categorical PRODUCT, rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType at the head of the Aggregate tower's product branch, the general shape behind the Rational numerator·denominator pair, the Complex real·imaginary pair, the Interval start·end pair, the Quantity magnitude·unit pair, and the unary law's Frame output·effect product) and IS its physical facet prim:physicalFacet prim:TupleProductEncoding (yang — the in-order concatenation of the member encodings over the prim:CompositeCarrier product carrier, octet-granular and endian-neutral, descending through prim:CompositeCarrier → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor), joined by prim:gluedBy prim:TupleRealizesProduct, the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow that carries the yin into the yang bearing the lossless-framing prim:NoEffect. REFLEXIVE SEAL (spec §9d): because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual — the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Tuple, so the round-trip prim:physicalFacet → prim:interpretAs → prim:formalFacet returns prim:Tuple itself, a TRUE IDENTITY, and the full reversibility loop physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet → realizesAs → encoding returns the chosen encoding. yin holds yang and yang holds yin; this is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility, sealed here for the general product with NO silm:isProvisional gap because Tuple is concrete and directly, exactly grounded on the concatenation-over-CompositeCarrier carrier — the fixed-arity product atom the AOB tensor block and every composite formal type of the floor ultimately grounds its member layout into." .

# =====================================================================================
# (c) The colimit taiji primitive — one glued atom = URI (yin) ⊕ UriUtf8 (yang).
# =====================================================================================
# formalFacet prim:URI ; physicalFacet prim:UriUtf8 ; gluedBy prim:realize_URI_Utf8.
# Seals reflexively: physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet = prim:URI (TRUE identity).

prim:prim_URI a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive URI (taiji: URI ⊕ UTF-8 interpreted identifier)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:URI ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:UriUtf8 ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_URI_Utf8 ;
    rdfs:comment "The Uniform-Resource-Identifier primitive: the colimit object of the twin-olog diagram (prim:URI ─realization ρ→ prim:UriUtf8), ONE glued atom that simultaneously IS its formal type (yin prim:formalFacet prim:URI, an ASCII-restricted global identifier, URI ⊂ URN) and IS its byte-carrier encoding (yang prim:physicalFacet prim:UriUtf8, an interpreted UTF-8 octet stream that byteDescends octet → nibble → bit to the prim:Bit floor), joined by prim:gluedBy prim:realize_URI_Utf8 (the Kleisli/Frame arrow whose typed cost is prim:EncodingLoss from percent-encoding and normalization). REFLEXIVE SEAL (spec §9d): because prim:UriUtf8 prim:interpretAs prim:URI, the mutual round-trip physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet returns prim:URI — a TRUE identity — rather than closing to prim:URN as an atom built on the inherited prim:UrnUtf8 carrier would. TRANSITIVE-GROUNDING LINK: URI remains dual-grounded two ways — directly by this atom, and transitively through its ancestor prim:URN (URI ⊂ URN in formal.ttl, URN realized by prim:realize_URN_Utf8) — an ancestral grounding STEP B now MATERIALISES as the real edge prim:agp_URI_URN (prim:ancestralViaArrow prim:sub_URI_URN, prim:ancestralViaTransport prim:rt_sub_URI_URN, prim:ancestralLandsIn prim:URN_PhysicalEncoding = rho(prim:URN)), so the once-prose 'also grounded through the ancestor' is now a resolvable graph fact; so the spec §9a self/ancestor/descendant coverage holds while the round-trip is still a genuine identity; URI's own descendants prim:IRI and prim:QName are taxonomic subtypes of URI but each carries its OWN direct grounding (prim:realize_IRI_Utf8 / prim:realize_QName_Utf8 onto prim:IriUtf8 / prim:QNameUtf8, each interpreting reflexively back to prim:IRI / prim:QName, with its own prim:prim_IRI / prim:prim_QName), so the URI ancestry gives them only an additional §9a coverage path, not their grounding mechanism. No silm:isProvisional: URI is resolvable and fully sealed." .

# =====================================================================================
# (c) The colimit Primitive atom for prim:URN — the glued taiji (formal ⊕ physical).
#     ONE atom that simultaneously IS prim:URN (yin) and IS the UTF-8 ByteVector encoding
#     (yang), glued by the realization ρ above. Reflexive round-trip:
#         physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet
#       = prim:URN_PhysicalEncoding → prim:URN → prim:URN   (TRUE IDENTITY).
# =====================================================================================

prim:URN_Primitive a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive URN (taiji: URN ⊕ UTF-8 octet ByteVector)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:URN ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:URN_PhysicalEncoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:URN_Realization ;
    rdfs:comment "The Uniform-Resource-Name primitive — the colimit object of the twin-olog diagram (prim:URN ─ρ→ prim:URN_PhysicalEncoding), the taiji atom of this fragment. It is not two linked things but ONE glued atom: its prim:formalFacet is yin (prim:URN, the load-bearing Resource/Uniform/Name root of the Identifier tower and the addressing substrate for the S/O/P towers, a direct subclass of prim:FormalType), its prim:physicalFacet is yang (prim:URN_PhysicalEncoding, the interpreted UTF-8 octet name laid into a prim:ByteVector of ≤128 octets that descends prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor), and prim:gluedBy names the Kleisli/Frame arrow prim:URN_Realization that carries yin into yang bearing the typed prim:EncodingLoss cost. REFLEXIVE SEAL (spec §9d): because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual — the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:URN, so the round-trip prim:physicalFacet → prim:interpretAs → prim:formalFacet returns prim:URN itself, a TRUE IDENTITY, and the full reversibility loop physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet → realizesAs → encoding returns the chosen encoding; yin holds yang and yang holds yin. Because URN is the tower root, its descendants prim:URI, prim:IRI and prim:QName are taxonomic subtypes of URN; each nonetheless carries its OWN direct grounding (its own realizesFrom realization onto its own dedicated reflexive carrier that interprets back to itself — prim:UriUtf8 / prim:IriUtf8 / prim:QNameUtf8 — plus its own primitive prim:prim_URI / prim:prim_IRI / prim:prim_QName), so sealing URN gives them only an additional §9a ancestral coverage path, NOT their grounding mechanism. This is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split ↔ consolidated render reversibility, sealed here for URN with NO silm:isProvisional marker because URN is directly and resolvably grounded on the UTF-8 byte carrier — a concrete identifier root, not a genuinely undecidable Präriehund gap." .

# ---- (c) the colimit taiji atom: the glued Unit primitive ---------------------------
# The one glued thing that simultaneously IS prim:Unit (yin) and IS prim:UnitTerminalEncoding
# (yang), joined by prim:UnitRealizesTerminal. Because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual: the
# physical facet's prim:interpretAs closes back to prim:Unit, so
# physicalFacet -> interpretAs -> formalFacet returns prim:Unit exactly — a TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:UnitPrimitive a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Unit (taiji: Unit ⊕ zero-width empty product)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Unit ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:UnitTerminalEncoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:UnitRealizesTerminal ;
    rdfs:comment "The terminal-Unit primitive — the colimit object of the twin-olog diagram (prim:Unit ─ρ→ prim:UnitTerminalEncoding), one glued taiji atom rather than two linked fields. Its prim:formalFacet is yin prim:Unit (the terminal object of the formal category, the type with exactly one information-free value, the empty product and nullary Tuple, the identity for the categorical product and the monadic unit's natural home); its prim:physicalFacet is yang prim:UnitTerminalEncoding (the zero-width empty-product byte carrier it IS, the unary law's registered empty byte-vector occurrence, whose carrier descends prim:ByteVector -> prim:Octet -> prim:Nibble -> prim:Bit to the width-1 Bit floor); and prim:gluedBy names the ρ arrow prim:UnitRealizesTerminal that carries yin into yang bearing the prim:NoEffect effect. Because the gluing is a colimit it is MUTUAL: the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Unit, so the reversibility seal physicalFacet -> interpretAs -> formalFacet returns the primitive's own formal type — a TRUE IDENTITY round-trip, fully sealed, not a provisional proxy that would land on a different formal type. It is deliberately the DUAL of the Void primitive: same zero byte width, but Unit is inhabited by exactly one value and glued with prim:NoEffect, whereas Void is inhabited by none and glued with prim:Uninhabited. prim:Unit is a concrete, canonical, directly-realizable terminal leaf of the Algebraic-constructor tower, so this atom carries NO silm:isProvisional (spec §9a/§9b/§9d): the seal closes losslessly and byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor, the base unit of the whole primitive floor." .

# =====================================================================================
# (c) The colimit Primitive atom — one glued taiji (formal (+) physical)
# =====================================================================================
# formalFacet (yin) + physicalFacet (yang) + gluedBy (the rho arrow). The physicalFacet's
# prim:interpretAs closes back to the formalFacet, so the mutual round-trip is a TRUE
# IDENTITY: physicalFacet -> interpretAs == formalFacet == prim:UnitOfMeasure (spec §9d).
prim:prim_UnitOfMeasure a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive UnitOfMeasure (taiji: UnitOfMeasure (+) UINT16 registry index)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:UnitOfMeasure ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:UnitOfMeasureUint16Index ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:realize_UnitOfMeasure_Uint16Index ;
    rdfs:comment "The unit-of-measure primitive — the colimit object of the twin-olog diagram (prim:UnitOfMeasure --rho--> prim:UnitOfMeasureUint16Index), one glued taiji atom rather than two linked fields. Its prim:formalFacet is yin (the mathematical type prim:UnitOfMeasure it IS, a named reference magnitude for a prim:Dimension, parent of prim:Currency); its prim:physicalFacet is yang (the UINT16 registry-index encoding it IS); and prim:gluedBy names the rho Kleisli/Frame arrow prim:realize_UnitOfMeasure_Uint16Index that carries yin into yang with prim:NoEffect. Because the gluing is a colimit it is MUTUAL: the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:UnitOfMeasure, so yin holds yang and yang holds yin, and the reversibility seal physicalFacet -> interpretAs -> formalFacet returns prim:UnitOfMeasure exactly — a TRUE IDENTITY round-trip (spec §9d), not a proxy landing on prim:Natural. DESCENDANT SUBTYPING (spec §9a, taxonomic not grounding): prim:Currency rdfs:subClassOf prim:UnitOfMeasure is a taxonomic subtype (a currency IS a unit of measure), but prim:Currency carries its OWN direct grounding — its own realization prim:realize_Currency_Iso4217Alpha onto its own dedicated ISO-4217 reflexive carrier prim:CurrencyIso4217Alpha (whose prim:interpretAs closes back to prim:Currency) and its own primitive prim:prim_Currency; the UnitOfMeasure ancestry gives Currency only an additional §9a coverage path, NOT its grounding mechanism, while this atom self-grounds UnitOfMeasure directly. This mutual-colimit round-trip is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split (+) consolidated render reversibility." .

# ---- (c) The colimit prim:Primitive atom: Vector ⊕ VectorDenseArrayEncoding, glued by ρ ----------
# The taiji: Vector (yin) ⊕ VectorDenseArrayEncoding (yang), glued by the ρ above. The physical
# facet's interpretAs closes back onto prim:Vector, so the seal is a reflexive TRUE IDENTITY.
prim:VectorPrimitive a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Vector (colimit taiji: Vector ⊕ dense fixed-length equal-width array)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Vector ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:VectorDenseArrayEncoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:VectorRealizesDenseArray ;
    rdfs:comment "The vector primitive — the colimit object over the twin-olog diagram (prim:Vector ─realization ρ→ prim:VectorDenseArrayEncoding) for the single formal type Vector, the taiji atom taiji.ttl did not previously carry for Vector. It is not two linked things but ONE glued atom that simultaneously IS its formal facet prim:formalFacet prim:Vector (yin — the fixed-length, homogeneous, equal-width ordered array, rdfs:subClassOf prim:List in the Aggregate tower, the pinned-length subclass of prim:List, the rank-1 case of prim:Tensor, and the physical home of the AOB tensor/aggregate block's rank-1 layer) and IS its physical facet prim:physicalFacet prim:VectorDenseArrayEncoding (yang — the dense, fixed-length, equal-width array of the member encodings over the prim:ByteVector ordered-byte-vector carrier: contiguous equal-width element encodings abutted in index order with no count prefix, no per-element tag, and no terminator, directly index-addressable at offset k × element-width, octet-granular and little-endian, descending through prim:ByteVector → prim:Octet → prim:Nibble → prim:Bit to the width-1 floor), joined by prim:gluedBy prim:VectorRealizesDenseArray, the ρ Kleisli/Frame arrow that carries the yin into the yang bearing the lossless-framing prim:NoEffect. REFLEXIVE SEAL (spec §9d): because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual — the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Vector, so the round-trip prim:physicalFacet → prim:interpretAs → prim:formalFacet returns prim:Vector itself, a TRUE IDENTITY (it lands on Vector, not on its supertype prim:List, nor on prim:Tensor, nor a bare prim:ByteVector), and the full reversibility loop physicalFacet → interpretAs → formalFacet → realizesAs → encoding returns the chosen encoding. yin holds yang and yang holds yin; this is the per-primitive instance of the unary law's split↔consolidated render reversibility, sealed here for the fixed-length equal-width array with NO silm:isProvisional gap because Vector is concrete and directly, exactly grounded on the dense-equal-width-run-over-ByteVector carrier — the canonical vector atom the AOB tensor block's rank-1 layer and every fixed-length homogeneous byte/numeric array of the floor grounds its member layout into." .

# ---- (c) the colimit taiji atom: the glued Void primitive ---------------------------
# The one glued thing that simultaneously IS prim:Void (yin) and IS prim:VoidEmptyEncoding
# (yang), joined by prim:VoidRealizesEmpty. Because the gluing is a colimit it is mutual: the
# physical facet's prim:interpretAs closes back to prim:Void, so
# physicalFacet -> interpretAs -> formalFacet returns prim:Void exactly — a TRUE IDENTITY. This
# atom also fills the gap left by taiji.ttl, which carries prim_Unit but no prim_Void.
prim:VoidPrimitive a prim:Primitive ;
    rdfs:label "Primitive Void (taiji: Void ⊕ empty 0-byte encoding, uninhabited)" ;
    prim:formalFacet prim:Void ;
    prim:physicalFacet prim:VoidEmptyEncoding ;
    prim:gluedBy prim:VoidRealizesEmpty ;
    rdfs:comment "The initial-Void primitive — the colimit object of the twin-olog diagram (prim:Void ─ρ→ prim:VoidEmptyEncoding), one glued taiji atom rather than two linked fields. Its prim:formalFacet is yin prim:Void (the initial object of the formal category, the type with NO values, the empty coproduct and nullary Sum, the dual of the terminal prim:Unit; the unique-and-vacuous function OUT of Void is the categorical mirror of the unique function INTO Unit); its prim:physicalFacet is yang prim:VoidEmptyEncoding (the empty 0-byte carrier it IS, the unary law's registered empty byte-vector occurrence, whose carrier descends prim:ByteVector -> prim:Octet -> prim:Nibble -> prim:Bit to the width-1 Bit floor); and prim:gluedBy names the ρ arrow prim:VoidRealizesEmpty that carries yin into yang bearing the prim:Uninhabited effect. Because the gluing is a colimit it is MUTUAL: the physical facet's prim:interpretAs points back to prim:Void, so the reversibility seal physicalFacet -> interpretAs -> formalFacet returns the primitive's own formal type — a TRUE IDENTITY round-trip, fully sealed, not a provisional proxy that would land on a different formal type. The seal being total is consistent with the type being uninhabited: reflexive closure is a fact about the naming (interpretAs = formalFacet = prim:Void), independent of the number of inhabitants. It is deliberately the DUAL of the Unit primitive: same zero byte width, but Void is inhabited by NONE and glued with prim:Uninhabited, whereas Unit is inhabited by exactly one value and glued with prim:NoEffect. prim:Void is a concrete, canonical, directly-realizable initial leaf of the Algebraic-constructor tower, so this atom carries NO silm:isProvisional (spec §9a/§9b/§9d/§9f): it is the ONE honest edge of the floor, realized as the empty encoding with a typed prim:Uninhabited effect so universality stays total without a dodge, and it byte-descends to the width-1 prim:Bit floor, the base unit of the whole primitive floor." .

# =====================================================================================
# STEP C — LITERAL YONEDA: yo, rho AS A NATURAL TRANSFORMATION, THE YONEDA POINT
# =====================================================================================
# The panel residual and Directive 2 ("tighter yoneda realization of ologs of ologs,
# turtles all the way") asked that the realization ρ be closed as LITERAL YONEDA. STEP A
# made the formal olog a thin category and materialised the representable presheaves
# prim:repr_<A> = Hom(-, A) (the object action of the Yoneda embedding yo). STEP B made ρ a
# genuine FUNCTOR ON ARROWS (prim:RealizationTransport rho(f): rho(A)->rho(B)) and
# materialised the ancestral grounding paths. STEP C closes the loop, ADDING structure and
# deleting no claim (enrich-never-strip):
#
#   1. yo AS A FUNCTOR: the object map prim:yonedaObject (A -> yo(A) = prim:repr_<A>, 60
#      objects) and the ARROW map prim:YonedaArrow prim:yo_<f> (yo(f): yo(A)->yo(B), one per
#      SubtypeArrow generator, 59). Because the category is thin, arrow<->natural-transformation
#      is a BIJECTION Hom(A,B) ~ Nat(yo(A),yo(B)) (Yoneda fullness, checkable finite form), and
#      A -> repr_<A> is injective (Yoneda faithfulness: distinct types, distinct representables).
#   2. ρ AS A NATURAL TRANSFORMATION rho: yo => R (R the physical realization functor): a
#      per-object component prim:RhoComponent prim:rhoNat_<A> (yo(A) -> rho(A), 60), and for
#      EVERY subtype arrow f a prim:NaturalitySquare prim:natsq_<f> whose top yo(f) and bottom
#      rho(f)=RealizationTransport are related by the vertical components, prim:natCommutes true.
#      The square commutes because the thin formal category and its physical-carrier image each
#      hold at most one arrow per ordered pair, so both composite paths are the SAME arrow; the
#      corners are pinned so the commutation is teeth-checkable (STEP D q_naturality_commutes).
#   3. THE YONEDA POINT (unary law tie): each representable carries its universal element
#      prim:yonedaPoint = prim:id_<A> (the reflexive element of Hom(A,A)); prim:YonedaEvaluation
#      prim:yeval_<A> evaluates yo(A) at that point and yields rho(A) = the Frame output carrier,
#      the literal content of "realization = Yoneda evaluation at the representable". The Frame
#      IS the Yoneda point (docs/unary-byte-frame-law.md: "The Frame remains the physical Yoneda
#      point"), recorded as prim:Frame prim:isYonedaPoint true.
#   4. OLOGS OF OLOGS / TURTLES (Directive 2): each representable prim:repr_<A> is re-internalised
#      as itself an olog object (prim:OlogObject) of the presheaf olog prim:presheafOlog — an olog
#      whose objects are themselves ologs, turtles all the way down.
#
# These Yoneda nodes are deliberately NOT rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType and their properties
# never range over prim:PhysicalEncoding / prim:Realization / prim:Primitive / prim:Composition,
# so DualGroundingShape, EncodingShape, RealizationShape, PrimitiveShape and KleisliCompositionShape
# never fire on them and the floor stays green; STEP D adds the teeth (q_yoneda_faithful,
# q_yoneda_full, q_naturality_commutes, prim:YonedaShape, prim:FunctorTransportShape).
# =====================================================================================

# ---- Classes of the literal-Yoneda layer -------------------------------------------
prim:YonedaArrow a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "YonedaArrow" ;
    rdfs:comment "The image under the Yoneda embedding yo of one arrow of the thin formal category: a natural transformation yo(f): yo(A) -> yo(B) between the two representable presheaves Hom(-, A) and Hom(-, B), given by post-composition with the formal arrow f: A -> B. A YonedaArrow records prim:yonedaArrowOf (the formal arrow it is the yo-image of), prim:yonedaArrowDom (yo(A) = prim:repr_<A>) and prim:yonedaArrowCod (yo(B) = prim:repr_<B>). It is what promotes the Yoneda embedding from a bare object map (type -> representable) to a genuine FUNCTOR ON ARROWS. Because the formal category is thin, the correspondence (arrow A -> B) <-> (natural transformation yo(A) -> yo(B)) is a bijection Hom(A,B) ~ Nat(yo(A),yo(B)) — the checkable finite form of Yoneda fullness — so exactly one YonedaArrow exists per SubtypeArrow generator and it is the top edge of that arrow's naturality square." .

prim:RhoComponent a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "RhoComponent" ;
    rdfs:comment "A component at one object of the natural transformation rho: yo => R, where yo is the Yoneda embedding of the formal olog into presheaves and R is the physical realization functor. A RhoComponent is the genuine arrow rho_A: yo(A) = prim:repr_<A> -> rho(A) = the canonical realization carrier of A, recorded with prim:rhoComponentAt (the object A), prim:rhoComponentSource (yo(A)) and prim:rhoComponentTarget (rho(A)). It is realization-as-Yoneda-evaluation: applying the component is exactly evaluating the representable at its Yoneda point (the identity element of Hom(A,A)), which the Yoneda lemma identifies with the value R(A) = the carrier. Each RhoComponent is the vertical edge of every naturality square whose formal arrow has A as domain or codomain, and the commutation of those squares is what makes rho a NATURAL transformation rather than a bare object-indexed family." .

prim:NaturalitySquare a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "NaturalitySquare" ;
    rdfs:comment "A commuting square witnessing that rho: yo => R is natural over one subtype arrow f: A -> B of the thin formal category. Its four edges are genuine morphisms: TOP prim:natYonedaArrow = yo(f): yo(A) -> yo(B) (a prim:YonedaArrow); BOTTOM prim:natTransport = rho(f): rho(A) -> rho(B) (the STEP B prim:RealizationTransport); LEFT prim:natComponentDom = rho_A: yo(A) -> rho(A); RIGHT prim:natComponentCod = rho_B: yo(B) -> rho(B) (both prim:RhoComponent). It carries prim:natOfArrow (the formal arrow f the square is over) and prim:natCommutes true. The square commutes because the thin formal category and its physical-carrier image each hold at most one arrow per ordered pair, so the two composite paths yo(A) -> rho(B) (down-then-across and across-then-down) are literally the SAME arrow. The corner agreement (yonedaArrowDom(top) = rhoComponentSource(left), transportCod(bottom) = rhoComponentTarget(right), and so on) is pinned so the commutation is a teeth-checkable graph fact, not a decorative flag." .

prim:YonedaEvaluation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "YonedaEvaluation" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Yoneda evaluation of a representable presheaf at its Yoneda point: for an object A it evaluates yo(A) = Hom(-, A) at the universal element (the identity of Hom(A,A)) and yields the value R(A) = the canonical realization carrier of A. By the Yoneda lemma Nat(Hom(-, A), R) is naturally isomorphic to R(A), the isomorphism being exactly this evaluation-at-the-identity. A YonedaEvaluation records prim:evalPresheaf (the representable yo(A)), prim:evalAtPoint (the Yoneda point prim:id_<A>), prim:evalYields (rho(A), the byte-carrier that is the frameOutput of A's realization Frame) and prim:evalComponent (the prim:RhoComponent that realizes the evaluation). It is the literal materialisation of 'realization = Yoneda evaluation at the representable', tying the physical Frame target of rho — which the unary law names the Yoneda point — to the categorical evaluation." .

prim:OlogObject a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "OlogObject" ;
    rdfs:comment "An object of an olog-of-ologs: an olog considered as itself an object at the next level up, the materialisation of Directive 2's 'ologs of ologs, turtles all the way'. In this floor the representable presheaves prim:repr_<A> = Hom(-, A) are re-internalised as OlogObjects of the presheaf olog prim:presheafOlog: each representable is not only the Yoneda image of a tower object but is itself an olog (a presheaf on the formal olog is a diagram of sets, i.e. an olog) and therefore an object of the meta-olog. This is the tighter Yoneda realization the maintainer asked for — the Yoneda embedding lands the formal olog inside PSh(FormalOlog), whose objects are ologs, and that presheaf olog is itself an OlogObject, so the tower of ologs recurses (turtles all the way down). It is deliberately NOT rdfs:subClassOf prim:FormalType, so the tower dual-grounding law is never triggered on a representable." .

prim:PresheafOlog a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "PresheafOlog" ;
    rdfs:comment "The olog-of-ologs into which the Yoneda embedding yo lands the formal olog: PSh(FormalOlog), the category of presheaves on the thin formal category, whose objects are ologs (a presheaf is a Set-valued diagram, i.e. an olog) and among which the representables prim:repr_<A> = Hom(-, A) sit as the image of yo. There is one such meta-olog individual, prim:presheafOlog, and every representable prim:inPresheafOlog it; being itself an olog, the presheaf olog is also an prim:OlogObject, so the construction recurses — Directive 2's turtles all the way down. It is the ambient category in which rho's naturality squares and the Yoneda evaluations live, the tighter Yoneda realization of ologs of ologs." .

# ---- Properties of the literal-Yoneda layer ----------------------------------------
prim:yonedaObject a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "yonedaObject" ; rdfs:domain prim:FormalType ; rdfs:range prim:RepresentablePresheaf ;
    rdfs:comment "The object action of the Yoneda embedding yo, as a forward edge: prim:yonedaObject maps each FormalType object A to its representable presheaf yo(A) = Hom(-, A) = prim:repr_<A> (whose inverse edge prim:representableOf is authored in formal.ttl). The map A -> yo(A) is injective by construction (one representable per object, distinct IRIs), the object-level witness of Yoneda faithfulness. Together with the arrow action prim:YonedaArrow it exhibits yo as a genuine functor FormalOlog -> PSh(FormalOlog)." .

prim:yonedaPoint a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "yonedaPoint" ; rdfs:domain prim:RepresentablePresheaf ; rdfs:range prim:IdentityArrow ;
    rdfs:comment "The universal element (Yoneda point) of a representable presheaf: prim:repr_<A> prim:yonedaPoint prim:id_<A>, the reflexive identity element of the hom-set Hom(A, A). By the Yoneda lemma every natural transformation out of the representable Hom(-, A) is determined by its value at this one element, so it is the point at which Yoneda evaluation happens. The unary law names the Frame the physical Yoneda point; here the identity arrow is the categorical Yoneda point whose evaluation yields the realization carrier (the Frame output), tying the two readings together." .

prim:yonedaArrowOf a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "yonedaArrowOf" ; rdfs:domain prim:YonedaArrow ; rdfs:range prim:FormalArrow ;
    rdfs:comment "Names the formal arrow whose Yoneda image a prim:YonedaArrow is: yo(f) prim:yonedaArrowOf f. In a thin category the assignment f -> yo(f) is a bijection between Hom(A, B) and the natural transformations Nat(yo(A), yo(B)), so this back-edge is the witness of Yoneda fullness in checkable finite form — every natural transformation between representables comes from exactly one formal arrow, recorded here. Reading yonedaArrowOf recovers, for any natural transformation of representables, the precise subtyping fact it is the image of." .

prim:yonedaArrowDom a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "yonedaArrowDom" ; rdfs:domain prim:YonedaArrow ; rdfs:range prim:RepresentablePresheaf ;
    rdfs:comment "The domain of a prim:YonedaArrow yo(f): yo(A) -> yo(B): the source representable presheaf yo(A) = prim:repr_<A>, equal to prim:yonedaObject applied to the domain of the formal arrow the YonedaArrow is prim:yonedaArrowOf. It is one corner of the naturality square (its top-left), pinned so that the square's commutation — yonedaArrowDom(top) must equal rhoComponentSource(left) — is a teeth-checkable graph fact." .

prim:yonedaArrowCod a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "yonedaArrowCod" ; rdfs:domain prim:YonedaArrow ; rdfs:range prim:RepresentablePresheaf ;
    rdfs:comment "The codomain of a prim:YonedaArrow yo(f): yo(A) -> yo(B): the target representable presheaf yo(B) = prim:repr_<B>, equal to prim:yonedaObject applied to the codomain of the formal arrow the YonedaArrow is prim:yonedaArrowOf. It is one corner of the naturality square (its top-right), pinned so that yonedaArrowCod(top) must equal rhoComponentSource(right) — the corner agreement that makes the square commute checkably." .

prim:rhoComponentAt a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "rhoComponentAt" ; rdfs:domain prim:RhoComponent ; rdfs:range prim:FormalType ;
    rdfs:comment "The object at which a prim:RhoComponent is the component of the natural transformation rho: yo => R. For rho_A: yo(A) -> rho(A) this is A. Together with prim:rhoComponentSource (yo(A)) and prim:rhoComponentTarget (rho(A)) it types the component as a genuine arrow from the representable to the realization carrier of the same object, so the family of components is indexed by the objects of the formal olog exactly as a natural transformation requires." .

prim:rhoComponentSource a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "rhoComponentSource" ; rdfs:domain prim:RhoComponent ; rdfs:range prim:RepresentablePresheaf ;
    rdfs:comment "The source of a prim:RhoComponent rho_A: the representable presheaf yo(A) = prim:repr_<A>, the domain of the natural-transformation component at A. It is the vertical-edge corner of the naturality square (left edge's top for the domain object, right edge's top for the codomain object), pinned equal to prim:yonedaArrowDom / prim:yonedaArrowCod of the square's top edge so the square commutes checkably." .

prim:rhoComponentTarget a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "rhoComponentTarget" ; rdfs:domain prim:RhoComponent ; rdfs:range prim:PhysicalObject ;
    rdfs:comment "The target of a prim:RhoComponent rho_A: the canonical realization carrier rho(A) = prim:rhoObject(A), the codomain of the natural-transformation component at A and the value R(A) that Yoneda evaluation at A's Yoneda point yields. Its range is prim:PhysicalObject (the STEP B plumbing supertype of encodings and the ByteVector apex) so range-inference never mistypes a carrier as a prim:PhysicalEncoding. It is the vertical-edge corner pinned equal to prim:transportDom / prim:transportCod of the square's bottom edge, closing the naturality square." .

prim:natOfArrow a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "natOfArrow" ; rdfs:domain prim:NaturalitySquare ; rdfs:range prim:SubtypeArrow ;
    rdfs:comment "The formal subtype arrow f: A -> B that a prim:NaturalitySquare is the naturality witness over. The square asserts that the component family rho makes the square with top yo(f), bottom rho(f), and vertical components rho_A and rho_B commute; natOfArrow names the one generator arrow it is built over, so a query can pair each subtyping fact with its naturality square and confirm the transformation is natural on every generator of the thin category." .

prim:natYonedaArrow a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "natYonedaArrow" ; rdfs:domain prim:NaturalitySquare ; rdfs:range prim:YonedaArrow ;
    rdfs:comment "The TOP edge of a prim:NaturalitySquare: the prim:YonedaArrow yo(f): yo(A) -> yo(B), the image of the square's formal arrow under the Yoneda embedding. Together with the bottom prim:natTransport (rho(f)) and the vertical prim:natComponentDom / prim:natComponentCod it forms the four edges of the commuting square whose commutation makes rho a natural transformation." .

prim:natTransport a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "natTransport" ; rdfs:domain prim:NaturalitySquare ; rdfs:range prim:RealizationTransport ;
    rdfs:comment "The BOTTOM edge of a prim:NaturalitySquare: the STEP B prim:RealizationTransport rho(f): rho(A) -> rho(B), the image of the square's formal arrow under the realization functor R. Its transportDom and transportCod are pinned to rhoObject of the arrow's domain and codomain, so they coincide with the prim:rhoComponentTarget of the square's left and right vertical components — the corner agreement that makes the square commute checkably (STEP D q_naturality_commutes)." .

prim:natComponentDom a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "natComponentDom" ; rdfs:domain prim:NaturalitySquare ; rdfs:range prim:RhoComponent ;
    rdfs:comment "The LEFT edge of a prim:NaturalitySquare: the prim:RhoComponent rho_A at the domain object A of the square's formal arrow, the vertical arrow yo(A) -> rho(A). Its prim:rhoComponentSource must equal the top edge's prim:yonedaArrowDom (both yo(A)) and its prim:rhoComponentTarget must equal the bottom edge's prim:transportDom (both rho(A)), the two corner equalities on the domain side that the commutation check reads." .

prim:natComponentCod a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "natComponentCod" ; rdfs:domain prim:NaturalitySquare ; rdfs:range prim:RhoComponent ;
    rdfs:comment "The RIGHT edge of a prim:NaturalitySquare: the prim:RhoComponent rho_B at the codomain object B of the square's formal arrow, the vertical arrow yo(B) -> rho(B). Its prim:rhoComponentSource must equal the top edge's prim:yonedaArrowCod (both yo(B)) and its prim:rhoComponentTarget must equal the bottom edge's prim:transportCod (both rho(B)), the two corner equalities on the codomain side that the commutation check reads." .

prim:natCommutes a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "natCommutes" ; rdfs:domain prim:NaturalitySquare ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:comment "Flags that a prim:NaturalitySquare commutes: the two composite paths yo(A) -> rho(B) through the square (down-then-across rho(f) after rho_A, and across-then-down rho_B after yo(f)) are the same arrow. In this thin formal category and its physical-carrier image there is at most one arrow per ordered pair, so commutation holds definitionally on the nose and this flag is true for every square; the flag is the checkable trace STEP D's q_naturality_commutes reads alongside the pinned corner equalities that prove the two paths share endpoints." .

prim:evalPresheaf a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "evalPresheaf" ; rdfs:domain prim:YonedaEvaluation ; rdfs:range prim:RepresentablePresheaf ;
    rdfs:comment "The representable presheaf a prim:YonedaEvaluation evaluates: yo(A) = Hom(-, A) = prim:repr_<A>. Yoneda evaluation of this presheaf at its Yoneda point (the identity of Hom(A,A)) yields R(A) = the realization carrier, so evalPresheaf names which representable the evaluation is of, the object end of the natural isomorphism Nat(Hom(-, A), R) ~ R(A)." .

prim:evalAtPoint a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "evalAtPoint" ; rdfs:domain prim:YonedaEvaluation ; rdfs:range prim:IdentityArrow ;
    rdfs:comment "The Yoneda point at which a prim:YonedaEvaluation evaluates its representable: the identity arrow prim:id_<A>, the universal element of Hom(A, A). Evaluation at this one element is the isomorphism Nat(Hom(-, A), R) ~ R(A) of the Yoneda lemma, so evalAtPoint records exactly where the evaluation happens; the unary law names the Frame the physical Yoneda point, and this identity arrow is its categorical counterpart." .

prim:evalYields a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "evalYields" ; rdfs:domain prim:YonedaEvaluation ; rdfs:range prim:PhysicalObject ;
    rdfs:comment "The carrier a prim:YonedaEvaluation yields: rho(A) = R(A) = the canonical realization byte-carrier of A, which is also the frameOutput coordinate of A's realization Frame. This is the literal content of 'realization = Yoneda evaluation at the representable': evaluating yo(A) at its Yoneda point produces exactly the physical Frame target of rho. Its range is prim:PhysicalObject so range-inference never mistypes the carrier as a prim:PhysicalEncoding." .

prim:evalComponent a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "evalComponent" ; rdfs:domain prim:YonedaEvaluation ; rdfs:range prim:RhoComponent ;
    rdfs:comment "The natural-transformation component that realizes a prim:YonedaEvaluation: the prim:RhoComponent rho_A: yo(A) -> rho(A) whose application IS the evaluation of yo(A) at its Yoneda point. Linking the evaluation to its component makes explicit that realization (the component of rho) and Yoneda evaluation (at the identity point) are the same arrow, the categorical identity the maintainer's tighter-Yoneda directive asks for." .

prim:inPresheafOlog a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "inPresheafOlog" ; rdfs:domain prim:OlogObject ; rdfs:range prim:PresheafOlog ;
    rdfs:comment "Places an prim:OlogObject inside the olog-of-ologs prim:presheafOlog: each representable prim:repr_<A> prim:inPresheafOlog prim:presheafOlog, marking it an object of the presheaf category PSh(FormalOlog) into which the Yoneda embedding lands. Since that presheaf olog is itself an olog, it is itself an prim:OlogObject, so the membership recurses — Directive 2's ologs of ologs, turtles all the way down." .

prim:isYonedaPoint a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "isYonedaPoint" ; rdfs:domain prim:FormalType ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:comment "Marks the formal type that IS the Yoneda point of the unary law: prim:Frame prim:isYonedaPoint true. The unary byte-frame law states 'The Frame remains the physical Yoneda point for one such input stream' — the typed product Frame(output, effect) around a byte stream is the point at which a callable's one input maps to its output and effect. This flag records that identification in the graph, tying the categorical Yoneda point (the identity element at which representables are evaluated to yield realization carriers) to the physical Frame the whole floor's realization monad is the Kleisli category of." .

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Yoneda object map, universal element (Yoneda point), rho natural-transformation
# component, and Yoneda evaluation — one per object of the thin formal category (60 objects).
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
prim:Bag prim:yonedaObject prim:repr_Bag .
prim:repr_Bag a prim:OlogObject ; prim:inPresheafOlog prim:presheafOlog ; prim:yonedaPoint prim:id_Bag ;
    rdfs:comment "The representable presheaf prim:repr_Bag = Hom(-, prim:Bag) = yo(prim:Bag) re-internalised in taiji.ttl as itself an OLOG OBJECT (prim:OlogObject) of the presheaf olog prim:presheafOlog — Directive 2's ologs-of-ologs, turtles all the way down: an olog whose objects are themselves ologs. Its prim:yonedaPoint is the identity arrow prim:id_Bag, the reflexive element of Hom(prim:Bag, prim:Bag) and the universal element the Yoneda lemma pins every natural transformation out of this representable to; evaluating this representable at that Yoneda point yields the realization carrier prim:BagMultisetEncoding = rho(prim:Bag) (see prim:yeval_Bag). Its materialised hom-set (formal.ttl prim:homSetHasArrow, 2 arrow(s) with codomain prim:Bag) is preserved unchanged; this file only adds the olog-object and Yoneda-point structure the literal Yoneda embedding needs." .
prim:rhoNat_Bag a prim:RhoComponent ; prim:rhoComponentAt prim:Bag ; prim:rhoComponentSource prim:repr_Bag ; prim:rhoComponentTarget prim:BagMultisetEncoding ;
    rdfs:label "rho component at prim:Bag: yo(prim:Bag) -> rho(prim:Bag)" ;
    rdfs:comment "The component at object prim:Bag of the natural transformation rho: yo => R (the Yoneda embedding into presheaves, transformed into the physical realization functor R). It is the genuine arrow rho_Bag: yo(prim:Bag) = prim:repr_Bag -> rho(prim:Bag) = prim:BagMultisetEncoding, mapping the representable presheaf of prim:Bag to its canonical realization carrier. This component IS realization-as-Yoneda-evaluation: applying it is exactly evaluating the representable prim:repr_Bag at its Yoneda point prim:id_Bag (the identity element of Hom(prim:Bag, prim:Bag)), which the Yoneda lemma identifies with the value R(prim:Bag) = the carrier prim:BagMultisetEncoding = the frameOutput of prim:Bag's realization Frame. It is the left/right vertical edge of every naturality square whose arrow has prim:Bag as domain/codomain, and its commutation with the transported subtype arrows is what makes rho a NATURAL transformation rather than a bare object-indexed family." .
prim:yeval_Bag a prim:YonedaEvaluation ; prim:evalPresheaf prim:repr_Bag ; prim:evalAtPoint prim:id_Bag ; prim:evalYields prim:BagMultisetEncoding ; prim:evalComponent prim:rhoNat_Bag ;
    rdfs:label "yo(prim:Bag) @ prim:id_Bag = prim:BagMultisetEncoding" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Yoneda evaluation of the representable presheaf prim:repr_Bag = yo(prim:Bag) at its Yoneda point prim:id_Bag: by the Yoneda lemma Nat(Hom(-, prim:Bag), R) is naturally isomorphic to R(prim:Bag), the isomorphism being evaluation at the identity element prim:id_Bag of Hom(prim:Bag, prim:Bag). Here R is the physical realization functor, so this evaluation yields prim:evalYields prim:BagMultisetEncoding = rho(prim:Bag), the canonical byte-carrier of prim:Bag. This is the literal content of realization = Yoneda evaluation at the representable: the physical Frame target of rho (prim:BagMultisetEncoding, the frameOutput coordinate of prim:Bag's realization) is recovered as the value of the representable at the Yoneda point, and the unary law names exactly that Frame the Yoneda point (docs/unary-byte-frame-law.md: 'The Frame remains the physical Yoneda point'). The evaluation is realized by the natural-transformation component prim:evalComponent prim:rhoNat_Bag." .

